The attached image, Figure 3, demonstrates that Nano-11 induces a transient inflammatory response at the injection site and is efficiently taken up by recruited immune cells. Panel a illustrates the kinetics of inflammatory cell infiltration, showing a peak in Mac-2+ macrophages and antigen-presenting cells around Day 2, with neutrophils appearing early (6h) and rapidly declining thereafter. Panel b's confocal images reveal that fluorescently labeled Nano-11 is internalized by MHCII+, Ly6C+, and Mac-2+ cells at the injection site, indicating active phagocytosis and consistent colocalization with these immune cell markers from 6h to Day 7. This supports Nano-11's role in local immunostimulation and antigen delivery.